WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.804
Use-after-free prevention is a design activity, but tooling helps catch mistakes earlier.

2
00:00:05.804 --> 00:00:10.780
AddressSanitizer can detect many memory lifetime and access errors during instrumented execution.

3
00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:13.682
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer can help with related undefined behavior.

4
00:00:13.682 --> 00:00:20.315
Compiler warnings, static analysis, and focused diagnostics can point reviewers toward suspicious ownership or invalidation patterns.

5
00:00:20.315 --> 00:00:24.876
Tools only observe the paths that are built, instrumented, and exercised.

6
00:00:24.876 --> 00:00:29.851
A sanitizer run that passes does not prove every lifetime is safe.

7
00:00:29.851 --> 00:00:36.070
Tests should target lifecycle-sensitive scenarios: object destruction, container mutation, error exits, cancellation, unregistration, ownership

8
00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:38.972
handoff, and callback execution after shutdown begins.

9
00:00:38.972 --> 00:00:46.435
Regression tests are especially valuable when fixing a stale access bug so the same pattern does not return.

10
00:00:46.435 --> 00:00:49.337
Code review should ask practical lifetime questions.

11
00:00:49.337 --> 00:00:50.996
Who owns this object?

12
00:00:50.996 --> 00:00:55.142
Can any pointer, reference, iterator, view, or callback outlive it?

13
00:00:55.142 --> 00:00:58.044
Can a container modification invalidate stored access?

14
00:00:58.044 --> 00:01:00.946
Does a moved object still get used?

15
00:01:00.946 --> 00:01:03.849
Is .release() followed by an immediate owner?

16
00:01:03.849 --> 00:01:05.922
Are raw pointer boundaries documented?

17
00:01:05.922 --> 00:01:10.068
These questions turn lifetime safety into a repeatable review habit.

18
00:01:10.068 --> 00:01:12.970
Prioritize tests that exercise the boring edges.

19
00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:22.091
Destroy objects while work is pending, resize containers after taking views, move owners before callbacks run, and force failure paths that usually

20
00:01:22.091 --> 00:01:23.335
receive less attention.

21
00:01:23.335 --> 00:01:29.968
These scenarios are valuable because lifetime bugs often hide in cleanup and rarely used recovery logic.

22
00:01:29.968 --> 00:01:33.700
Make the test describe the lifetime rule it protects.

23
00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:38.675
Findings should be treated as engineering defects, not one-off cleanup chores.

24
00:01:38.675 --> 00:01:44.479
Track them, add tests, improve API contracts, and consider whether similar patterns exist elsewhere.

25
00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:49.040
Tools support judgment, but clear ownership design remains the strongest defense.
